{"title":"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of its inscribed circle. The internal bisectors of the angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a9874ea56cf8fec1bd246793224fbcfaad17ab3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> meet the opposite sides in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6aaa7deb4cf5fc2e91449ded6cc714345cc4aa9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A^\\prime,B^\\prime,C^\\prime$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"19\" /> respectively. Prove that \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/a/1cac703999912eafd59c3d0fd73015b43e231b49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {1}{4} &lt; \\frac {AI\\cdot BI\\cdot CI}{AA^{\\prime }\\cdot BB^{\\prime }\\cdot CC^{\\prime }} \\leq \\frac {8}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"211\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/d/35d7283b26607b45b6573a26b5e7c792290608e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\prod\\frac{AI}{AA^\\prime}=\\prod\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{IA^\\prime}{IA}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"170\" height=\"45\" />.  From Van Aubel's Theorem, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/990d71ed2db32638a46beb4534fa7b100953bfb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{IA}{IA^\\prime}=\\frac{AB^\\prime}{B^\\prime C}+\\frac{AC^\\prime}{C^\\prime B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"152\" height=\"38\" /> which from the Angle Bisector Theorem reduces to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/d/96d4209669eac1f10c51e350d900c2655750dd40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{b+c}{a}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"40\" height=\"37\" />.  We find similar expressions for the other terms in the product so that the product simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/5/3d59d08c48e8027afc01246d131b60de8ca1e655.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\prod\\frac{1}{1+\\frac{a}{b+c}}=\\prod\\frac{b+c}{a+b+c}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"209\" height=\"45\" />. Letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/e/cee0f6f7096ff929f675fa85b0ef11141e6bbafc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=x+y,b=y+z,c=z+x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"239\" height=\"16\" /> for positive reals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/accc80fdf164cef264f56a82b6f9f6add320fe05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y,z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"13\" />, the product becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/d/45dc172db779161673d883f3f27abbbf3d67e27d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{8}\\prod\\frac{x+2y+z}{x+y+z}=\\frac{1}{8}\\prod\\left(1+\\frac{y}{x+y+z}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"326\" height=\"43\" />.  To prove the right side of the inequality, we simply apply AM-GM to the product to get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/a/4aabdea0b2579751a8d61674c1ce45a6cefc9fe4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{8}\\prod\\left(1+\\frac{y}{x+y+z}\\right)\\le\\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{\\sum1+\\frac{y}{x+y+z}}{3}\\right)^3=\\frac{8}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"400\" height=\"56\" />\n</p><p>To prove the left side of the inequality, simply multiply out the product to get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/f/67f7cc6bc6b1fc8cd8b62dff75d8c1de911cc4e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{8}\\left(1+\\sum\\frac{y}{x+y+z}+\\sum\\frac{yz}{(x+y+z)^2}+\\frac{xyz}{(x+y+z)^3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"448\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/f/cef76f7b327f13dbac508f73465a688bb48b88cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt;\\frac{1}{8}\\left(1+\\sum\\frac{y}{x+y+z}\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"235\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>as desired.\n</p><p>Remark: To prove the right side of the inequality, a quicker way might be to use Gergonne's and AM-GM.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO\" title=\"1991 IMO\">1991 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First Question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1991_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1991 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017182817\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.032 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 84/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 868/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 52/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3300/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.041      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    7.041      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7222:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017182817 and revision id 291139. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}